WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.488
A package describes the system
on the day it was generated.

2
00:00:03.488 --> 00:00:06.212
Continuous monitoring
keeps that day recent.

3
00:00:06.212 --> 00:00:08.138
The law already sets the floor.

4
00:00:08.138 --> 00:00:11.021
FISMA requires testing
no less than annually,

5
00:00:11.021 --> 00:00:13.374
and there's no federal three-year rule.

6
00:00:13.374 --> 00:00:19.188
OMB's Circular A-130 tells agencies to move
to ongoing authorization when a system

7
00:00:19.188 --> 00:00:22.693
is eligible, and once
monitoring is mature enough,

8
00:00:22.693 --> 00:00:25.600
a review frequency replaces the end date.

9
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:30.063
NIST is clear that controls which can't
be automated must still be assessed.

10
00:00:30.063 --> 00:00:34.657
The program's cadence, as its own
design: monthly, regenerate the results

11
00:00:34.657 --> 00:00:39.175
and the plan of action, and report
posture to the authorizing official.

12
00:00:39.175 --> 00:00:43.174
Quarterly, review each provider's
Ongoing Certification Report;

13
00:00:43.174 --> 00:00:48.062
FedRAMP's rule says agencies must, and they
should name a senior official to do it.

14
00:00:48.062 --> 00:00:52.124
Annually, the independent assessor
re-assesses every control.

15
00:00:52.124 --> 00:00:55.655
Agencies must also notify
FedRAMP when a report raises

16
00:00:55.655 --> 00:00:59.338
a concern likely to lead to
rescinding the authorization.

17
00:00:59.338 --> 00:01:02.424
And vendor posture scores
aren't assessment evidence.

18
00:01:02.424 --> 00:01:05.975
A score measures progress against
a vendor's recommendations.

19
00:01:05.975 --> 00:01:10.750
An assessment result says whether an
objective is satisfied, and on what evidence.
